WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.212
UPDATE changes values in rows that already exist
Because it modifies stored

00:00:05.238 --> 00:00:08.688
data we want to target the correct row as carefully
as possible

00:00:09.562 --> 00:00:13.638
The example begins with a SELECT It looks for the
patient whose

00:00:13.662 --> 00:00:18.675
MRN is MRN one zero zero three This preview step
confirms which

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:23.325
row matches before anything is changed Then the
UPDATE statement targets the

00:00:23.338 --> 00:00:29.212
same patients table SET age equals thirty-five
describes the new value The

00:00:29.238 --> 00:00:34.050
WHERE clause uses the same MRN condition that we
just previewed Finally

00:00:34.262 --> 00:00:39.675
RETURNING shows the patient ID MRN name and updated
age Notice the

00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:44.050
pattern preview with SELECT then update using the
same stable predicate then

00:00:44.062 --> 00:00:48.412
inspect with RETURNING Why do we prefer an MRN or
primary key

00:00:48.438 --> 00:00:52.425
instead of a name Names are not always unique and
names can

00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:57.550
change Stable identifiers make it less likely that
we update multiple people

00:00:57.562 --> 00:01:02.288
accidentally Run the example and compare the preview
row with the returned

00:01:02.312 --> 00:01:06.800
updated row The identifying values should stay the
same while the age

00:01:06.812 --> 00:01:11.262
changes Whenever you write an UPDATE ask two
questions before running it

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:14.475
which rows can match this WHERE condition and how
many rows do

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:18.300
I expect to change If the answer is unclear go back
to

00:01:18.312 --> 00:01:19.738
SELECT and preview first
